  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a square matrix\n- $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n- $A$ is an orthogonal matrix",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
